# Tightbeam {tag} installation sheet

Before requesting installation, require a successful isolated rehearsal of this exact bundle at the supported database, WAL, archive and writer scale. The record must name this bundle's `SHA256SUMS` digest and measure the whole action, outage and failure boundaries. If it is absent or failed, record the exact gap and hold the install request. Do not substitute a smaller fixture or a prior job's result.

The package `{package}` comes from source `{source}` and has SHA-256 `{package_sha}`. Its provenance names the exact source commit, workflow and package. The maintenance job has SHA-256 `{job_sha}`. The launcher has SHA-256 `{launcher_sha}`. The exact manifest has SHA-256 `{manifest_sha}`; `SHA256SUMS` verifies all bundle files before staging. The supported installed predecessor is Tightbeam {old_version} build {old_build} with schema stamp `{old_schema}`. The expected post-migration stamp is `{new_schema}`. Any build, schema, path, unit, package or hash mismatch stops before service control.

{purpose_summary}

Expected database effect: {schema_effect} Other first-start effects: {startup_effects} If this release changes an adapter or other file under the base directory, the manifest must name each required preflight file and its installed SHA-256. Include its saved-original and restoration limit in the exact install request.

{rehearsal_summary}

## Compatibility before the gateway restart

Before asking for the exact install approval, record the currently installed gateway build, schema, rule and identity state, and the release's supported transition from each of them. Record every registered satellite's effective `tightbeam` executable path, version and digest, and compare its required gateway interface with this release. The gateway requires an exact CLI version match for dispatch. A gateway package does not update a satellite CLI. Record the gateway version pins of browser and mobile clients that use it, including clients that load another client, and the effect of this release on each.

For every known mismatch, the whole-action install request must identify either a same-window update using published platform assets with exact hashes, paths, ordering and restoration limits, or the named satellite or client that will be unavailable until its separately approved repair. Include every affected host and client in the approval scope; approval for this gateway alone does not authorize changes to them. This sheet does not change the gateway's current exact-version policy. If the inventory, supported rule/identity transition, required interface, published update asset or impact cannot be established, warn the operator which facts are unknown and which gateway, satellite or client functions may be affected; do not present uncertainty as compatibility or silently take those functions offline. Recheck the recorded identities and effective executable paths immediately before the approved run, and disclose any changed or newly unknown impact. The launcher's exact predecessor build, schema, path and package checks remain in force.

Before approval, record current activity and confirm that the next scheduled state backup will not overlap the measured outage window. This installation needs its own exact whole-action yes. Prepare one persistent interactive terminal pane with the verified bundle directory selected and the complete `sudo -- ./install.sh` command visible before asking the operator to act. After approval, the operator starts that command in the same pane and enters one sudo password there. Do not unlock sudo in a different terminal or leave a second agent command between password entry and installation. There is no second RUN prompt. The launcher checks the installed predecessor, verifies the package and stages it under `{root}`. A transient maintenance unit stops `{unit}` once, proves there is no database writer, backs up the database and required archives into `{backup}`, verifies their integrity and hashes, selects the new build, starts the service once, and checks the migration stamp, service and version. The private durable result log is `{log}`.

The affected paths are `{root}`, `{base}`, `{backup}` and `{log}`. The backup copies configuration, identity, adapter and home files that may contain credentials; those bytes stay in owner-only backup files and are not included in the rehearsal report. A backup on the same filesystem does not protect against disk loss. Before the new boot, the old build can be restarted only after its selector, predecessor schema and no-writer state are proved. The old source database does not receive a full integrity scan during the outage; the copied database receives a quick check and foreign-key check before selection of the new build. Each database quick check has a one-hour cap, and each foreign-key check has a ten-minute cap. If backup verification fails before the new boot, the job checks the old selector, schema and no-writer state before one old-service start. After the new boot may have migrated the database, do not switch back to the old executable or restore data automatically. A database restore needs separate approval and loses writes made after the backup.

Use the accompanying rehearsal record for the measured duration, outage range, stop conditions and remaining limits. A disconnected terminal is resolved by reading the durable log and transient unit state; do not start another job from an uncertain terminal result.
